ath :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340648/field-3/f8bea3e43e510040edd05c66961a62722a5463ada634a718d698f04377265cae" :answer/id #uuid "52b05a3a-a24f-5413-8182-bfe9a49455cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340648/field-3/3a561f2e8a663b29d619072a47e2c2a24db22c445f24586ce19db22ca4fc991c" :answer/id #uuid "59dfed63-346f-50a1-879d-aec9b9fe3302" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340648/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "abfdea54-ba49-5b3e-8ec4-fe03d16ca16e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-340648/field-3/bf97266dd2c2b3ea01f35e08316b9471790121e89e976ea03940b5f307a3ff95" :answer-field/choices ["q-340648/field-3/bf97266dd2c2b3ea01f35e08316b9471790121e89e976ea03940b5f307a3ff95" "q-340648/field-3/9f865a5b3c8d23b94016564f8f3deb2a775ee6797c2a773fb612552cb3670474" "q-340648/field-3/6aa4f0e9e9909b3a6214988374551e69ff4b16821605d4eeb87846d5b9b1310c" "q-340648/field-3/cad19b82ee1c4a96158f71ab30a985cea48ebbf2df819aeeffb5a294f116de32" "q-340648/field-3/f8bea3e43e510040edd05c66961a62722a5463ada634a718d698f04377265cae" "q-340648/field-3/3a561f2e8a663b29d619072a47e2c2a24db22c445f24586ce19db22ca4fc991c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340648" :question/id #uuid "7dc09a0e-31fd-55ae-8496-e8a1f65e9cde" :question/math-academy-id "q-340648" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}+6x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{3}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={b}_{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{3} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-340648/field-1" "q-340648/field-2" "q-340648/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-340648"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-340418"] :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{5}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{a}_{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-10\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={a}_{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{5} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-340647/field-1/6b81038f21071b18a893fbfe9c60ed65e5da22d59fecf930cfb3f01052d27963" :answer/id #uuid "ec6013c9-7e66-5134-8ad4-1e802761ad81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340647/field-1/f747e6aafcdb87436a558571346282eb4051ded801e4bca3ee1d2022a3dc2f88" :answer/id #uuid "18a6b8fb-fc71-569b-8c7b-f9ba32fa2389" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340647/field-1/66ceb467500c3acb7c77a43b169853dca7b98e9520329958c96b55f4cbd73931" :answer/id #uuid "51afa5e1-db21-5f0c-b264-8237511bf486" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340647/field-1/736cdeceaf586c6b36abf150bd70697fdc6878764c725cf6fbb7cc4a3da8811b" :answer/id #uuid "183d18a3-a70e-5f8a-9079-3ff65f88b193" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)⟩}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340647/field-1/c569793c4c87163d98be9c0c6552b87ea1bbfbf3cf0e4b580e957da9ecadcf68" :answer/id #uuid "1a11681c-9f02-5c7e-a11d-537b9bb86694" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2