} {:db/id "q-340662/field-2/82f0679237d603c9b88d77fbc6f240065a24a3716631fab4d9f58426a7f011e5" :answer/id #uuid "03efb26a-2460-5688-86ad-aa05eb5e5765" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340662/field-2/167ebf11c001034e2cbb5c82fd03c6c789b33fae94a34649b1567e72676329e0" :answer/id #uuid "08be0311-b8a9-5c52-ad43-dcde04095648" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340662/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "c414ab39-7de6-593a-8f44-518bde5d8c4c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-340662/field-2/b70aae751b2c8b3e9e8a13d794a4d2996d95172e6176a2e8db9c2f456750b489" :answer-field/choices ["q-340662/field-2/b70aae751b2c8b3e9e8a13d794a4d2996d95172e6176a2e8db9c2f456750b489" "q-340662/field-2/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" "q-340662/field-2/a319e2074b264fdb964c2b6eb43d32d763280e23b0599a55d545f0a58e6fea9e" "q-340662/field-2/82f0679237d603c9b88d77fbc6f240065a24a3716631fab4d9f58426a7f011e5" "q-340662/field-2/167ebf11c001034e2cbb5c82fd03c6c789b33fae94a34649b1567e72676329e0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340662/field-3/9156afa14745a0d77f03a5cf104846c690b43ca1a5bf775f1db4a5256791e182" :answer/id #uuid "405674ed-13de-581d-b036-81306aef7df5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340662/field-3/6a2681a196fa0e0ff21cd3207f5d02fb0977c54a48048076a570d377d8945250" :answer/id #uuid "f16beace-a78a-510c-84c4-77bb7ca374f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340662/field-3/fcfaa5a4af78322b2b2c596bdfa9fa112e2b9d421ca7a7325f18bba7240a2a9f" :answer/id #uuid "e1908470-9458-52ad-a8a0-d2c6b538ba11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340662/field-3/8d99129a4aeeb72d081ba428837205bab1445424ad0238e3eaade39d5d51f02f" :answer/id #uuid "a54c133c-962b-5568-a016-f0bac9e0bda2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340662/field-3/f00e027cabb65fa4f42d285793ce7560e28ec673476babb287cf74d67386cbcc" :answer/id #uuid "8a242fbd-275b-577f-9536-c10c4c6d7a8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340662/field-3/5e25c2acffb75dc8529b491749066584756815d7f4e46304c6cbc5a77d70622f" :answer/id #uuid "03b6037f-9cd6-52f3-b957-917eb611f528" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340662/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "549fff5d-30f8-53a7-bc0c-1f6faf082c6c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-340662/field-3/f00e027cabb65fa4f42d285793ce7560e28ec673476babb287cf74d67386cbcc" :answer-field/choices ["q-340662/field-3/9156afa14745a0d77f03a5cf104846c690b43ca1a5bf775f1db4a5256791e182" "q-340662/field-3/6a2681a196fa0e0ff21cd3207f5d02fb0977c54a48048076a570d377d8945250" "q-340662/field-3/fcfaa5a4af78322b2b2c596bdfa9fa112e2b9d421ca7a7325f18bba7240a2a9f" "q-340662/field-3/8d99129a4aeeb72d081ba428837205bab1445424ad0238e3eaade39d5d51f02f" "q-340662/field-3/f00e027cabb65fa4f42d285793ce7560e28ec673476babb287cf74d67386cbcc" "q-340662/field-3/5e25c2acffb75dc8529b491749066584756815d7f4e46304c6cbc5a77d70622f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340662" :question/id #uuid "02ca39fb-07ed-50d7-9c80-f756da5d6843" :question/math-academy-id "q-340662" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-6x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{4}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{4} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-340662/field-1" "q-340662/field-2" "q-340662/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-340662"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21787"] :question/problem "Let $F(x) = \\cos(2x)$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x \\in [0,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\pi) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f(x) = F(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R},$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in[0,\\pi).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi)=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi$-periodic, because for any $x\\in\\mathbb{R},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(x+\\pi ))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=f(0)=F(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\neq f(x)$ for $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21788"] :question/problem "$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x) g(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ defined above, compute $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(6x), \\cos(4x) \\rangle$ and determine if functions $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\sin(6x)\\cos(4x)\n=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\sin(10x)+\\sin(2x)\\big).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(1-1)-\\frac{1}{4}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ \n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21789"] :question/problem "Let $F(x) = 2x^2+5$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ to $x\\in \\mathbb R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n \\cos(nx) + b_n \\sin(nx)\\right).\n$$\n\nDetermine the integral expression for the coefficient $a_4.$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left( a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $a_0,$ $a_n,$ and $b_n$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\cos(4x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1, \\sin(x), \\cos(x), \\sin(2x), \\cos(2x), \\ldots$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\na_4 \\left\\langle \\cos(4x), \\cos(4x) \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={a}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2{x}^{2}+5)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
